Chapter: PlugEffectValueSubject

 An effect where Fortran wants a value: the four statement-shaped builtins in
 VALUE position, and a def that is both effectful and value-returning. Kept
 out of `codex/test` on purpose: that tree is a battery tier and this is a
 probe. Truth on x86-64: `one two three unit value discard 13`.

 The last shape before the print, `counted "discard"`, is the one Fortran
 has no legal form for at all: a statement that calls a function and throws
 the result away. It is here so the gap has a subject.

 Fortran has no expression statement, so a plug that lowers an effectful
 builtin to a Fortran STATEMENT is correct only where the emitter happens to
 be emitting a statement. Every shape below puts one somewhere else.

Section: Shapes

  bind-print : Text -> [Console] Integer
  bind-print (msg) = act
   r <- (print-line-uni msg)
   1
  end

  bind-error : Text -> [Console] Integer
  bind-error (msg) = act
   r <- (print-error-uni msg)
   2
  end

  branch-print : Boolean, Text -> [Console] Integer
  branch-print (c) (msg) = act
   r <- (if c then print-line-uni msg else print-error-uni msg)
   3
  end

 `shout` and `counted` are the discriminating pair: both are effectful, and
 only the second returns a value. Nothing in `codex/plugs/test-input/`
 defines a unit-returning effectful def, so the stock inputs cannot tell an
 emitter that asks what a def RETURNS from one that answers the same way
 for every effectful def.

  shout : Text -> [Console] Nothing
  shout (msg) = act
   print-line-uni msg
  end

  counted : Text -> [Console] Integer
  counted (msg) = act
   r <- (print-line-uni msg)
   7
  end

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
   a <- bind-print "one"
   b <- bind-error "two"
   c <- branch-print True "three"
   shout "unit"
   n <- counted "value"
   counted "discard"
   print-line-uni (show (a + b + c + n))
  end
